WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.314
Welcome to our training "Manage Your Experiments - Interpret Results for Listing Content You've

00:00:06.314 --> 00:00:06.800
Tested."

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.656
In this third video of the "Manage Your Experiments" series, we'll show you how to view, assess,

00:00:12.656 --> 00:00:16.100
and apply the results of an M-Y-E Test.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:22.343
If you haven't watched our earlier videos on planning and setting up an M-Y-E Test, we recommend

00:00:22.343 --> 00:00:25.300
viewing them before you continue with this final training.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:32.074
After setting up an M-Y-E Test and submitting it to Amazon for Validation, you can check the status

00:00:32.074 --> 00:00:35.800
of your experiment anytime on the M-Y-E home page.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:42.061
An active status means that your experiment is in progress, and that customers viewing your listing

00:00:42.061 --> 00:00:44.800
content are being split into two groups.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.300
One group sees your original A content each time it appears in the Amazon store.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.300
The other group sees your alternate B content.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:00.845
You can usually review updated results for an experiment every one to two weeks by viewing its details

00:01:00.845 --> 00:01:02.300
in M-Y-E.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:09.268
If you decide to review results before an experiment ends, remember that they can fluctuate because

00:01:09.268 --> 00:01:15.365
results represent actual customer behavior, including any atypical traffic over a short period of

00:01:15.365 --> 00:01:15.800
time.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.240
Results are more reliable when they're viewed after an experiment has run to significance, or if

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.300
it's run for at least eight to ten weeks.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:31.538
We provide three types of M-Y-E results and present aggregate data if your test includes more than

00:01:31.538 --> 00:01:32.300
one ASIN.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:38.420
At the top of the results page, you'll see a percentage for the probability that one version of

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.800
your content is better than the other.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:47.300
Underneath your probability percentage, you'll see a breakdown of metrics for your A and B content.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:53.800
You can compare total units sold and total sales associated with the appearance of each version.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:01.400
You can also view the sample size for each version of your content, which we use to calculate two

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:01.800
percentages.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.007
The first is conversion rate, which is the number of customers who viewed a version of your detail

00:02:08.007 --> 00:02:11.800
page content and made a purchase divided by the sample size.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:18.479
The second percentage is units sold per unique visitor, which is the total units sold after customers

00:02:18.479 --> 00:02:22.800
viewed a version of your content divided by the sample size.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.191
The last result type you'll see for a completed M-Y-E test is the projected one-year impact of your

00:02:29.191 --> 00:02:29.800
winning content.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:36.757
We calculate this impact by determining the average daily units sold and the average daily sales

00:02:36.757 --> 00:02:39.800
for both your losing and winning content.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:45.800
We then subtract the losing averages from your winning ones and multiply by 365.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:52.550
These estimates can't take seasonality, price changes, and other factors into account, so they aren't

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.800
a guarantee of future sales.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.800
But they can help you understand how the results of your M-Y-E experiment might scale.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:07.411
You'll see a most likely column for your projected one-year impact, which shows the average outcome

00:03:07.411 --> 00:03:07.800
calculated.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:16.300
You'll also see best-case and worst-case columns which show high and low outcomes calculated with

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.800
95% statistical confidence.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.976
After reviewing the results of your experiment, you can publish your alternate content using standard

00:03:23.976 --> 00:03:24.800
listing tools.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:31.857
Or if you left the auto-publish option defaulted while setting up your M-Y-E test, we'll publish

00:03:31.857 --> 00:03:37.057
your alternate content automatically, as long as it performed significantly better than your existing

00:03:37.057 --> 00:03:37.800
listing content.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.800
This concludes our training and our series on M-Y-E.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.800
Thank you, and happy selling in the Amazon Store!
